# AI Test Recorder

Record a session on **any** web application (click, type an amount, etc.),
and generate a runnable **Behave + Playwright** framework: a `.feature` file,
a `steps.py`, and a Page Object with multi-strategy locators - then run it
with one click.

## Why LangGraph + LangChain

- **LangChain** wraps the model calls (Azure OpenAI or DeepSeek - both work
  through the same `ChatOpenAI`-style interface, see `agent/llm_factory.py`).
- **LangGraph** orchestrates the workflow as a small state machine, because
  it isn't a single prompt: `generate artifacts -> run tests -> (if a
  locator broke) self-heal -> run again`. That retry loop is what
  `agent/graph.py` implements. LangChain alone can do the individual calls,
  but the stateful retry loop is naturally a graph, not a linear chain.

The pipeline still works with **zero LLM keys configured** - it falls back
to heuristic element naming and simply reports failures without
self-healing. The LLM makes step names/Gherkin phrasing more natural and
adds the self-healing repair step; it isn't required for the core
record -> generate -> run loop.

## How locator accuracy is achieved

Recorders are only as good as the selectors they capture. This one never
relies on a single selector:

1. At record time (`recorder/recorder_injector.js`), every clicked/typed
   element gets **up to 7 independent locator candidates**: `data-testid`,
   `id`, `name`, `aria-label`, `placeholder`, text-based XPath, and a
   generated CSS/XPath path - each validated for uniqueness on the page
   before being kept.
2. `recorder/locator_strategy.py` ranks them by robustness.
3. The generated Page Object (`generator/templates/page.tmpl`) tries them
   **in order** at runtime, so if the top strategy breaks, it silently
   falls back to the next one.
4. Only if *every* stored strategy fails does it call the **self-healing
   agent** (`agent/self_healer.py`), which reads the live DOM, asks the LLM
   for a new selector, validates it resolves to exactly one element, and
   persists it back into `locators.json`.

## Navigation menu check (no recording, no hovering)

Tests every link of a site's menu - Personal Banking > Accounts > Saving
Account, and so on - without anyone recording or hovering anything.

**Use it:** Run Saved Recording tab -> "Create a navigation menu check" ->
pick the application (or type the address) -> Create -> Run recording.

**What a run does.** It reads the menu from the live page, then for each
link: goes back to the start page, opens the parent menus with the real
mouse, clicks the link and checks the page that opened. A link fails when:

- the page returns an HTTP error (404, 500 ...) or does not load
- the page loads but is an error page ("Page not found" ...) or blank
- clicking does nothing
- (after the first run) the item is gone from the menu, or its link changed

Links that open a new tab, download a file (PDF) or go to another site are
handled and noted. Cookie banners and welcome pop-ups are closed first.
The exit report has one row per menu link, each with its own screenshot.

**Baseline.** The first run saves the menu as it is; later runs are compared
with it. After an intended menu change, click "Reset baseline" and run once.

**Settings** (in the create form):

- *Menu selector* - leave blank; fill in a CSS selector (`nav`,
  `#main-menu`) only if the wrong list gets picked.
- *Fail a link if its menu does not open on hover* - off by default: the
  link is still checked and the row gets a note. Turn on to make a menu that
  will not open a failure.

**After a login.** The check starts from whatever page is open when its step
runs. To check a menu behind a login, record the login as usual, then add a
`MENU-CHECK` row at the end in "Edit steps".

**Limits.** One run stops starting new links ~90 seconds before
`AGENT_TEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (default 600) - raise that for very large
menus; unchecked links are reported as "Skipped". Menus are found through
`ul/li` lists and Bootstrap-style dropdowns in the page header/nav; a menu
that only appears after tapping a mobile "hamburger" button is not opened.

Code: `generator/templates/page.tmpl` (`check_navigation_menu`),
`generator/build.py` (`menu_check` action), `runner/run_recording.py`
(per-link report rows, baseline), `backend/api.py` (`/api/menu_check`).

### Recorded clicks inside hover menus

A recorded click on a menu item no longer needs recorded hover steps. If
the target exists but is hidden inside a closed menu, replay opens the
menus above it (found from the page, not from recorded pixel positions)
and then clicks. Recorded hover steps now also use the element's position
at replay time. Set `AUTO_OPEN_MENUS=false` to switch the auto-open off.

### Runs do not depend on the mouse pointer

Menus are opened without the pointer: the browser is told to treat the
parent items as hovered (the DevTools "force :hover" switch, sent over
CDP), and hover events are sent straight to script-driven menus. In a
visible browser the person's real cursor otherwise overrides an automated
pointer move, and the run only advances while someone holds the mouse on
the menu. Nobody needs to touch the mouse during a run now. Moving the
pointer is kept as the fallback (elements inside an iframe). Set
`MENU_OPEN_WITHOUT_MOUSE=false` to go back to pointer-only.

### Recorded hover steps are locator-driven

A recorded hover is replayed through the element's recorded locators:
find it (all recorded candidates, then self-healing), scroll it into view,
`locator.hover()`, then wait for the next step's element to become visible.
The pixel position stored at recording time is used only if no locator
matches anything. Code: `hover_element()` in `generator/templates/page.tmpl`.

### New tabs during recording

A click that opens a new tab is recorded in the right order, what the user
does inside the new tab is recorded, and they can close that tab or leave
it open and carry on in the first one - replay follows the same tabs. The
recording only ends on Save & Generate (or Stop): closing a tab, even the
first one, no longer ends it. `recorder/recorder.py` and
`recorder/recorder_injector.js` run inside the local agent, so the agent
has to be rebuilt/redeployed for this.

## Authentication, sessions & admin panel

The whole tool now sits behind login (JWT-based). Quick tour:

- **Login** - `POST /api/auth/login` with a username/ADID + password, issued
  by an admin (see below). Every login gets its own session row in the
  database (`sessions` table) - sessions are never shared between users.
- **First login / temp passwords** - accounts are created with a temp
  password and `must_change_password=true`. The frontend forces a redirect
  to `/change-password` (current/temp password + new password + confirm)
  until that's cleared.
- **5-minute idle auto-logout** - enforced in two places, deliberately:
  the frontend watches for mouse/keyboard/scroll activity and logs out
  locally after 5 idle minutes (with a warning banner beforehand), and the
  *backend* independently rejects any API call once a session has gone
  5 minutes without an authenticated request (`auth/dependencies.py`) -
  so this holds even if someone scripts around the frontend.
- **Admin panel** (`/admin/users`, `/admin/sessions`, `/admin/activity` in
  the frontend) - an admin can create/edit/deactivate/delete users, reset
  anyone's password, see who's online right now (and force-log them out),
  and browse/export every user's activity (recordings started, tests run,
  logins, profile changes, ...) as CSV.
- **Everyone's dashboard/profile** - `/dashboard` after login, and
  `/profile` to update your own name/email and review + export your own
  activity history.
- **SSO-ready** - `AUTH_PROVIDER=local` today; wiring up State Bank of
  India's SSO later is meant to be a single-function change
  (`auth/providers.py:authenticate_sso`) - see the docstring in
  `auth/config.py` for the intended integration shape. Session handling,
  the idle timeout, JWTs, and the admin/activity views don't need to
  change for that.

The very first admin account is seeded automatically on first run from
`ADMIN_USERNAME`/`ADMIN_TEMP_PASSWORD` in `.env` (defaults to
`admin` / `ChangeMe@123` - **change these before running anywhere but your
own machine**). Log in with it, change the password when prompted, then
create real accounts from the Users admin page.

**Hosting this for multiple people** (not just running it locally)? See
[`DEPLOYMENT_WINDOWS.md`](DEPLOYMENT_WINDOWS.md) - it covers running as
Windows Services, a reverse proxy with TLS, and how concurrent *recording*
specifically works once several people can be using the tool from their
own machines at once (short version: recording runs headless on the
server and is watched/controlled live through the browser - see
`backend/recording_sessions.py` and `recorder/recorder.py`).

### Just a few people on the same local network (no server needed)

Concurrent recording isn't tied to a real server deployment - it's
application logic that works the same wherever the backend runs. For a
small team on one office LAN, a full Windows Server setup is overkill;
run it on any one machine and point everyone else at it:

```bash
# on the host machine - bind to the LAN, not just localhost
uvicorn backend.api:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

```ini
# frontend/.env.local on the HOST machine - its own LAN IP, not "localhost"
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://192.168.1.50:8000
```

```bash
cd frontend && npm run build && npm run start
```

Everyone else on the network then opens `http://192.168.1.50:3000`.
Windows Firewall will prompt the first time another machine reaches
it - allow it for your private/domain network profile. This skips TLS,
a reverse proxy, and auto-restart-on-crash/reboot (all of which
`DEPLOYMENT_WINDOWS.md` adds) - fine for casual internal use, but the
tool goes down if that machine's terminal closes, sleeps, or reboots, and
`MAX_CONCURRENT_RECORDINGS` should be set lower than a real server's
(a laptop realistically handles maybe 2-4 concurrent live recordings, not
10-30).

## Setup

```bash
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate      # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
cp .env.example .env
```

Edit `.env`:
- Set `LLM_PROVIDER=azure` and fill `AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY` / `AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT` / `AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT`, **or**
- Set `LLM_PROVIDER=deepseek` and fill `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY`.
- Set `JWT_SECRET_KEY` to a long random value (not the placeholder) before
  running this anywhere but your own machine - see the auth section above
  for the rest of the auth-related `.env` keys.

Then install the Playwright browser driver files once:
```bash
playwright install msedge
```

By default this uses **Microsoft Edge** - you need Edge installed locally,
and Playwright drives it directly via its `channel="msedge"` option (the
`playwright install msedge` above just makes sure Playwright's own driver
files are present; it does not download a separate copy of Edge). To use
Chrome instead, set `BROWSER=chrome` in `.env` and run `playwright install
chrome` (Chrome must be installed locally in that case).

There's no more `EDGE_DRIVER_PATH`/`CHROME_DRIVER_PATH` to manage - Playwright
talks to the browser directly, so no separate driver executable is needed.

## Usage - UI (recommended)

The UI is a FastAPI backend (does the actual work, unchanged) + a **Next.js**
frontend (`frontend/`, the buttons). Run them in two terminals:

```bash
# terminal 1 - backend API
uvicorn backend.api:app --reload --port 8000

# terminal 2 - frontend
cd frontend
npm install        # first time only
cp .env.local.example .env.local   # first time only
npm run dev
```
Open `http://localhost:3000` in your browser - you'll land on `/login`.
Sign in with the seeded admin (or an account one creates for you), then:
1. Go to **Record & Run**, enter the target application's URL, click **Start Recording** - a browser window opens (Edge by default).
2. Interact with the app normally (click things, type an amount, submit, etc.).
3. Click **Save & Generate** - this stops the recording and writes:
   - `output/features/recorded.feature`
   - `output/features/steps/recorded_steps.py`
   - `output/pages/recorded_page.py` + `output/pages/locators.json`
4. Click **Run / Check** - runs the generated Behave suite against the same app and shows pass/fail + logs.

### Running a saved recording + exit report

The **"Run Saved Recording"** tab in the UI lets you re-run any
recording you already have in `recordings/`, without re-recording it,
always attributed to whoever is currently logged in (no more manual "run
as" picker - your session is the user):

1. Pick a saved recording from the dropdown (auto-populated from `recordings/*.json`).
2. Click **Run recording** - this regenerates its feature/steps/page files
   and executes them, then shows the **exit report**: overall
   Success/Failed status, a per-step Playwright/Behave log (with error
   messages for any failed step), the list of files produced, and a
   downloadable `.txt` report.

Every run is written to `transactions/transaction_<id>.json` (status,
per-step results, files produced) - see
[`database/README.md`](database/README.md#transactions--not-in-the-database)
for the format. The SQLite database in `database/` only ever holds
auth/session/activity data (who logged in, who ran what and when) - never
the recordings or their run results; see
[`database/README.md`](database/README.md) for that schema. The same flow
is available over the API (all endpoints below need
`Authorization: Bearer <token>` from `/api/auth/login`):

```bash
TOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/auth/login -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"username": "admin", "password": "ChangeMe@123"}' | python3 -c "import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)['access_token'])")

curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/recordings -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"

curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/run_recording -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"recording_file": "recordings/recording_20260814_103200.json", "headless": true}'
```

The backend also works standalone as a plain JSON API (e.g. for scripting
or a different frontend later) - remember the `Authorization` header:
```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/start -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"url": "https://www.saucedemo.com/"}'
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/stop_and_save -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/run -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"headless": false}'
```

## Usage - CLI

The CLI (`main.py`) talks to the recorder/generator/runner modules
directly, bypassing the API and its auth - it's meant for local
scripting, not the shared multi-user deployment described above.

```bash
python main.py record --url https://your-app.com
python main.py generate --recording recordings/recording_20260814_103200.json
python main.py run
```

Or generate **and** run through the LangGraph pipeline (with self-healing) in one step:

```bash
python main.py generate --recording recordings/recording_20260814_103200.json --run
```

## Generated output structure

```
output/
  features/
    recorded.feature
    environment.py         # Playwright browser setup/teardown, injected at generation time
    steps/
      recorded_steps.py
  pages/
    recorded_page.py       # BasePage + RecordedPage, locator-agnostic logic
    locators.json          # the actual selectors, editable / self-healable
  recorded_steps.xlsx      # every action + ALL candidate locators, for manual review
  reports/
    transaction_<id>_exit_report.txt   # human-readable exit report per run

transactions/
  transaction_<id>.json    # one file per "run a saved recording" execution -
                            # status, per-step results, files produced (see
                            # database/README.md#transactions--not-in-the-database)
```

### The Excel report (`output/recorded_steps.xlsx`)

Every recording generates a styled spreadsheet listing each step, its element name,
tag/type, the **primary** locator chosen, and the **full list of fallback locators**
that were captured (not just the one that got used) - useful for spotting exactly
why a specific element isn't resolving. Any row with **zero captured locators** is
highlighted red so it's immediately visible.

By default, values typed into `type="password"` fields are masked (`••••••••`) in
this report, since typed values are otherwise written to disk in plaintext. If you
need the real value visible (e.g. dummy/test-only credentials), call
`build_artifacts(recording, mask_passwords=False)` directly, or edit
`generator/build.py`'s call to `build_excel_report`.

### Radio buttons, checkboxes, and dropdowns

- **Radio/checkbox**: clicks on the visible label (a common pattern where the
  native `<input type="radio">` is visually hidden and a styled `<label>` sits on
  top) are resolved back to the underlying input via its `for` attribute or
  nested structure, so the locator always points at the real form control, not
  the label wrapper.
- **Native `<select>` dropdowns**: captured as a distinct `select` action (not
  lumped in with plain typing) and replayed using Playwright's
  `select_option()` (by visible label, falling back to by value) - this is
  the reliable way to drive a native dropdown; clicking/sending keys directly
  to a `<select>` is what usually causes "dropdown issues."
- **Custom (non-native) dropdowns** - div/li-based widgets that aren't a real
  `<select>` - fall back to: click to open, then click the option by its
  visible text.

### Keyboard-driven actions

Pressing a non-character key while focused on a field is captured as a
distinct `keypress` action (separate from typing), so flows that rely on the
keyboard - submitting with Enter instead of clicking a button, clearing a
field with Backspace, moving through an autocomplete list with the arrow
keys, selecting all with Ctrl+A, printing/saving with Ctrl+P/Ctrl+S, etc. -
are recorded and replayed correctly. Captured keys:

| Key pressed         | Recorded value | Replayed via Playwright's `press()`   |
|----------------------|----------------|----------------------------------------|
| Enter                | `ENTER`        | `"Enter"`                              |
| Backspace            | `BACKSPACE`    | `"Backspace"`                          |
| Delete               | `DELETE`       | `"Delete"`                             |
| Space                | `SPACE`        | `"Space"`                              |
| Tab                  | `TAB`          | `"Tab"`                                |
| Shift+Tab            | `SHIFT+TAB`    | `"Shift+Tab"`                          |
| Escape               | `ESCAPE`       | `"Escape"`                             |
| Arrow Up/Down/Left/Right | `ARROW_UP` / `ARROW_DOWN` / `ARROW_LEFT` / `ARROW_RIGHT` | `"ArrowUp"` / `"ArrowDown"` / `"ArrowLeft"` / `"ArrowRight"` |
| Home / End           | `HOME` / `END` | `"Home"` / `"End"`                     |
| F1 - F12             | `F1` ... `F12` | `"F1"` ... `"F12"`                     |
| Ctrl+A / Ctrl+P / Ctrl+S | `CTRL+A` / `CTRL+P` / `CTRL+S` | `"Control+A"` / `"Control+P"` / `"Control+S"` |
| Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V / Ctrl+X | *(see Copy/Paste below - not double-recorded as a keypress)* | `"Control+C"` / `"Control+V"` / `"Control+X"` via `press_key()` |

Each generated step calls the Page Object's generic `press_key(element_name,
key_name)` (e.g. `context.page.press_key("search_box_field", "ARROW_DOWN")`),
and the Gherkin step text names the actual key, e.g. `When the user presses
Arrow Down on "search_box_field"`. The original `press_enter(element_name)`
helper is still available and works the same as before (it's now a thin
wrapper around `press_key(element_name, "ENTER")`).

### Real-time copy/paste and Capture -> Store -> Reuse variables

Ctrl+C/Ctrl+V (and Ctrl+X) are handled separately from the generic keypress
capture above: the injected script listens for the browser's own `copy`,
`cut`, and `paste` events, which fire regardless of whether the user pressed
the keyboard shortcut or used a right-click menu, and records:

- **`copy`** - the text that was copied, plus the field it was copied *from*
  (its full locator set, same as any other action).
- **`paste`** - the text that was pasted, plus the field it was pasted *into*.

After recording stops, `recorder/variable_capture.py` walks the actions in
order and:

1. Names each `copy` after its source field's label, stripping filler words
   like "Generated"/"New" - e.g. a field labeled **"Generated Username"**
   becomes `{{username}}`, **"Generated Application ID"** becomes
   `{{application_id}}`.
2. Tags any *later* `paste` (or manually retyped `type`) whose value exactly
   matches a previously copied value with that same `{{name}}` placeholder.

This turns a flow like:

```
CTRL+C on the "Generated Username" field   -> {{username}}
...
CTRL+V into the "Username" field           -> reuses {{username}}
CTRL+C on the "Generated Application ID"   -> {{application_id}}
...
CTRL+V into the "Application ID" field     -> reuses {{application_id}}
```

into a generated feature file that reads `{{username}}` / `{{application_id}}`
instead of the literal strings captured at recording time, and generated
steps that:

- **Capture**: `context.page.capture_value(element_name)` reads the field's
  *current* value at playback time - preferring Playwright's
  `input_value()` / `text_content()` (works headless, no OS dependency), and
  only falling back to the browser's own clipboard (via
  `page.evaluate(...navigator.clipboard.readText()...)` - still
  100% Playwright, no external clipboard library) if the element exposes
  neither - then stores it in `context.variables`.
- **Reuse**: `context.variables.resolve("{{username}}")` (see
  `runner/variables.py`) looks up the value *actually generated during this
  run*, not the static value baked in when the recording was made, and that
  live value is what gets typed into the later field.

So if the target app generates a fresh username/application ID every run,
replay still enters the *current* run's values, not a stale recorded one.



## Notes / things to customize per real project

- The generated `Then the flow should complete without errors` step is a
  generic placeholder - replace it with a real assertion (e.g. "a success
  banner with text X is shown") once you know what a successful transaction
  looks like in your target app.
- Recorded typed values (e.g. the amount) are baked into the feature file
  as literals from the actual recording. Convert to a `Scenario Outline`
  with an `Examples` table if you want to replay the same flow with
  different amounts.
- This was built and packaged in a sandboxed environment without a live
  Chrome browser to record against, so the record -> replay loop hasn't
  been exercised against a real target app yet - run it locally and let me
  know if anything needs adjusting for your specific app's markup.
